<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · movement</p><h1 class="enc-title">Futurism</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1909">1909</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Futurism was an artistic and social movement launched by the Italian poet Filippo Tommaso Marinetti with his Futurist Manifesto of 1909. It celebrated speed, machines, violence and the modern city and rejected the past, influencing painting, sculpture, literature, architecture and music through artists such as Umberto Boccioni and Giacomo Balla. Many Futurists campaigned for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>'s entry into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a>, and Marinetti later allied himself with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fascism">Fascism</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/futurism.jpg" alt="Futurism" width="320" height="236" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Umberto Boccioni · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Dynamism_of_a_Biker_(1913)_by_Umberto_Boccioni.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
